t also the protracted legal battles that persisted for decades after the event. It is the third book by University of Michigan historian Heather Ann Thompson. Blood in the Water provides a complete history of the incidents at Attica reflecting a decade of research, including information from interviews, government records, personal correspondence, and legal documents, much of which has never been made public before. Thompson argues that the Attica uprising and New York state's response represented shifting American approaches to incarceration and policy. The reverberations of this watershed event has continued to influence America's prison system. Thompson served as lead historical consultant on the Showtime documentary Attica, released in 2021.","matchedBy":"infobox_book:high","confidence":"high","attribution":{"label":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood_in_the_Water%3A_The_Attica_Prison_Uprising_of_1971_and_Its_Legacy","license":"CC BY-SA","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"},"infobox":{"title":"Blood in the Water: The Attica Prison Uprising of 1971 and Its Legacy","author":"Heather Ann Thompson","publisher":"Pantheon","publicationDate":"August 2016","publicationPlace":"United States","pages":"752","isbn":"978-0-375-42322-2","language":"English","subject":"Attica Prison riot, Criminal Justice, Law, History, Race","genre":"Nonfiction"},"publisherEvidenceId":"publisher-evidence-wikipedia-blood-in-the-water-the-attica-prison-uprising-of-1971-and-its-legacy-heather-ann-thompson"},"sources":[{"id":"source-nonfiction-history-awards-by-imprint","label":"Nonfiction history awards by imprint seed workbook","url":"","accessedAt":"2026-07-22T18:06:52.029Z","confidence":"manual","note":"Initial import from the seed workbook. Publisher-level data is not inferred from imprints unless explicitly curated later."},{"id":"source-nonfiction-history-awards-by-imprint-5","label":"Columbia University Bancroft Prize previous-awards archive; 